UP-4 + UP-5 — WASM wrapper objects leaked when an operation threw
================================================================

SEVERITY: low (memory), but it degrades a long session.  FILES: 2 (+ specs).

THE ISSUE
---------
`libGD.js` is Emscripten: every `gd.Xxx` object you construct lives in the
WASM heap and must be released with `.delete()`. The convention in this
codebase is correct — `delete()` after use is everywhere — but it was being
called on the SUCCESS path only.

```js
// BEFORE — newIDE/app/src/Utils/Serializer.js (one of six such functions)
export function serializeToJSON(serializable, methodName = 'serializeTo', options) {
  const serializedElement = new gd.SerializerElement();
  serializable[methodName](serializedElement);   // <-- if this throws...
  const json = gd.Serializer.toJSON(serializedElement);
  serializedElement.delete();                     // <-- ...this never runs
  return json;
}
```

If `serializeTo` throws — a malformed object, a type confusion, an
out-of-memory condition inside the WASM — the wrapper is never deleted and
leaks for the rest of the session. The same shape existed in six functions in
`Serializer.js` (including a `VectorString` returned to the caller) and once
each for the exporter and the export options in
`LocalPreviewLauncher.launchPreview`:

```js
// BEFORE — LocalPreviewLauncher/index.js
const exporter = ...;
const previewExportOptions = new gd.PreviewExportOptions(project, outputDir);
exporter.exportProjectForPixiPreview(previewExportOptions);   // throws often
exporter.delete();
previewExportOptions.delete();
```

WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN "LOW"
---------------------------------
A preview export is the most failure-prone operation in the editor — a missing
GDJS folder, a bad resource path, an event that will not generate — and it is
the one a user retries. Ten failed exports leak ten exporters, and an
`Exporter` holds a whole serialized project graph. The user-visible symptom is
a preview tab that gets slower and then fails outright after a run of
unsuccessful attempts, which reads as "the IDE broke" rather than "a leak".

THE FIX
-------
`try { ... } finally { ... }` at all eight sites. In `Serializer.js` the six
functions become, in shape:

```diff
   const serializedElement = new gd.SerializerElement();
-  try {
-    serializable[methodName](serializedElement);
-    return gd.Serializer.toJSON(serializedElement);
-  } finally {
-    serializedElement.delete();
-  }
+  try {
+    serializable[methodName](serializedElement);
+    return gd.Serializer.toJSON(serializedElement);
+  } finally {
+    // Released whether or not the (un)serialization threw: an exception
+    // path that skipped this leaked a WASM allocation for the session.
+    serializedElement.delete();
+  }
```

and in the launcher the two hot-reload `SerializerElement`s get the same
treatment, plus an outer `finally` around the whole export:

```diff
+    // The exporter and the export options are WASM wrappers: they must still
+    // be deleted, so wrap the work in a try/finally.
+    try {
       exporter.exportProjectForPixiPreview(previewExportOptions);
       ...
+    } finally {
+      exporter.delete();
+      previewExportOptions.delete();
+    }
-  exporter.delete();
-  previewExportOptions.delete();
```

One incidental cleanup while in there: `Serializer.js` had a duplicated
invalid-JSON `catch` block, folded into a single helper with byte-identical
logging. It is called out here only so a reviewer is not surprised by it in the
diff.


TESTS
-----
- `Serializer.spec.js` uses a fake `gd` whose serialize/unserialize paths
  throw on demand, and asserts `delete` is still called. 14 cases across the
  six functions.
- The launcher spec asserts that a throwing export deletes BOTH the exporter
  and the export options, and that the success path deletes each exactly once
  (guarding against the double-delete that an over-eager `finally` would
  introduce).

This is a good test to copy upstream in spirit even where you do not apply the
patch: "the object is released on the throw path" is otherwise very hard to
notice, precisely because the leak is invisible until the heap runs out.
